\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\textwidth]{figures/cka_analysis.pdf}
\caption{CKA similarity between fine-tuned and pretrained encoder features (same checkpoints as Figure~\ref{fig:gradient_analysis}; cross-seed limitation applies). Vanilla and Full OCC preserve pretrained structure; Detach drifts catastrophically, especially in SigLIP middle layers.}
\label{fig:cka_analysis}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Representational drift confirms collapse.} Centered Kernel Alignment (CKA) between fine-tuned and pretrained features corroborates the gradient analysis (Figure~\ref{fig:cka_analysis}). Vanilla and Full OCC retain high similarity to pretrained representations (DINOv2 CKA: 0.968/0.964; SigLIP: 0.953/0.960). Detach OCC exhibits catastrophic representational drift (DINOv2: 0.539; SigLIP: 0.312), with SigLIP middle layers (blocks 10--16) dropping to 0.22--0.26. This completes a three-level evidence chain: gradient collapse (1--13\% norms) $\to$ representational drift (CKA $\downarrow$0.31--0.54) $\to$ behavioral degradation (CAR $-$3.4\,pp).

\textbf{Limitation.} Both gradient and CKA analyses use checkpoints from independent training runs with different random seeds (Full OCC seed 42, Detach seed 44, Vanilla seed 42). While the large effect sizes (gradient norms 1--13\% vs.\ 87--114\%; CKA 0.31--0.54 vs.\ 0.95--0.97) make seed confounds unlikely to explain the observed differences, same-seed replication is in progress. The behavioral results (3 seeds per condition, bootstrap CIs) provide the primary statistical evidence; the mechanistic analyses offer explanations consistent with those behavioral outcomes.

