Detach OCC falls 3.4\,pp below Vanilla despite identical architecture and forward computation to Full OCC. The auxiliary head's BatchNorm2d layers update running statistics at every forward pass regardless of gradient routing; with \texttt{stop\_gradient}, the encoder bears this statistical perturbation without compensating auxiliary gradients. This forward-pass interference makes Detach worse than Vanilla: the encoder absorbs the cost of the auxiliary pathway without receiving any training benefit. The TSR instability under Detach ($\sigma = 0.163$ vs.\ $< 0.050$ for all other conditions) confirms that gradient detachment destabilizes optimization broadly.

A discriminating test replaces the auxiliary head's BatchNorm2d layers with LayerNorm---which normalizes per-sample and does not maintain cross-batch running statistics---while keeping gradient detachment (\texttt{stop\_gradient}) unchanged. If Detach+LN recovers to Vanilla-level CAR ($\approx 0.780$), the 3.4\,pp deficit is a BatchNorm artifact; if it remains near Detach ($\approx 0.746$), gradient flow absence is the genuine cause. A mixed outcome (CAR between 0.746 and 0.780) would indicate both factors contribute.

\textit{[Detach+LN results pending (plan\_027, 3 seeds, expected $\sim$22:00 UTC 2026-05-23). Three scenario analyses are pre-written:]}

\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Scenario A} ($\sim$40\%): Detach+LN CAR $\approx$ Vanilla. The Detach deficit is a BN artifact; gradient detachment is safe with per-sample normalization. This would weaken the gradient-flow claim but yield a precise architectural guideline.
\item \textbf{Scenario B} ($\sim$45\%): Detach+LN CAR $\approx$ Detach. Gradient flow is genuinely necessary regardless of normalization. This strengthens the central finding and eliminates the BN confound.
\item \textbf{Scenario C} ($\sim$15\%): Detach+LN CAR between Detach and Vanilla. Both BN interference and gradient-flow absence contribute. This yields a nuanced dual-mechanism account.
\end{itemize}

